From 488c5a9c26d69c3e99ea9ab35ff096a84dbd97d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 9 May 2026 06:09:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] freedreno/a2xx: fix non-fast clear color on A22X (write
 to PS CONST[0])

The "solid" clear shader (freedreno_program.c:solid_fs) is:
    DCL CONST[0]
    MOV OUT[0], CONST[0]

It outputs whatever is in fragment-shader CONST[0] (ALU type-0 vec4
constant) as the fragment color. The A20X clear path correctly writes
the user's clear color to PS const slot 0 via CP_SET_CONSTANT type-0
at offset 0x480 (= PS_CONST_BASE * 4). On A22X this previously wrote
to REG_A2XX_CLEAR_COLOR - which is the *fast-clear* register that the
solid shader never reads.

Direct gl-capture experiments isolated this:

  - Same shader / same vertex data / same glClearColor(0.10, 0.20,
    0.30) on the same Adreno 220 hardware via the legacy webOS
    proprietary stack: produces correct dark-navy background.
  - Mainline freedreno + a2xx in sysmem mode (FD_DBG_SYSMEM): produces
    solid-blue background (CONST[0] held stale (0,0,1,?) from a
    previous draw or uninitialized context state).
  - Pixel-diff against the legacy webOS reference confirmed the only
    differences in sysmem mode are the background pixels, while the
    triangle interpolation itself was correct.

Match the A20X path: write the four float channels to the PS-const-0
slot the shader actually reads. The CLEAR_COLOR register only matters
for the fast-clear path (fd2_clear_fast), which is A20X-only anyway.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/fd2_draw.c | 36 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/fd2_draw.c b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/fd2_draw.c
index c7ee307d6d7..cc152eb9b11 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/fd2_draw.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/fd2_draw.c
@@ -576,9 +576,39 @@ fd2_clear(struct fd_context *ctx, enum fd_buffer_mask buffers,
       }
    } else {
       if (buffers & FD_BUFFER_COLOR) {
-         OUT_PKT3(ring, CP_SET_CONSTANT, 2);
-         OUT_RING(ring, CP_REG(REG_A2XX_CLEAR_COLOR));
-         OUT_RING(ring, pack_rgba(PIPE_FORMAT_R8G8B8A8_UNORM, color->f));
+         /*
+          * A22X clear color path.
+          *
+          * The "solid" clear shader (freedreno_program.c:solid_fs) is:
+          *   DCL CONST[0]
+          *   MOV OUT[0], CONST[0]
+          * i.e. it outputs whatever is in fragment-shader CONST[0]
+          * (ALU type-0 vec4 constant) as the fragment color.
+          *
+          * The A20X branch correctly writes the user's clear color to
+          * PS const slot 0 via CP_SET_CONSTANT type-0 at offset 0x480
+          * (= PS_CONST_BASE*4). On A22X this previously wrote to
+          * REG_A2XX_CLEAR_COLOR (the *fast-clear* register) instead -
+          * which the solid shader never reads. The fragment color
+          * therefore came out as whatever happened to be sitting in
+          * CONST[0] from a previous draw or context (the bug surfaced
+          * as a solid-blue background on a glClearColor(0.10, 0.20,
+          * 0.30) call - direct gl-capture confirmed via pixel-diff
+          * against the legacy webOS proprietary stack on the same
+          * hardware).
+          *
+          * Match the A20X path: write the four float channels to the
+          * same PS-const-0 slot the shader actually reads. The
+          * CLEAR_COLOR register would only matter for the fast-clear
+          * code path (which short-circuits via fd2_clear_fast above
+          * and is A20X-only anyway - see line 707).
+          */
+         OUT_PKT3(ring, CP_SET_CONSTANT, 5);
+         OUT_RING(ring, 0x00000480);  /* type=0 ALU, PS_CONST_BASE*4 */
+         OUT_RING(ring, color->ui[0]);
+         OUT_RING(ring, color->ui[1]);
+         OUT_RING(ring, color->ui[2]);
+         OUT_RING(ring, color->ui[3]);
       }
 
       if (buffers & (FD_BUFFER_DEPTH | FD_BUFFER_STENCIL)) {
-- 
